  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**  \n   **Central idea**: We minimize the Erds minimum overlap integral constant \\(C_5\\) by optimizing an anti-palindromic step function using dual annealing, which balances exploration and exploitation. This approach leverages symmetry to reduce dimensionality (\\(212\\) variables for \\(425\\) points) and the linear Diophantine method to approximate the grid integral efficiently.  \n   **Potential improvements**: Anti-palindromic functions maintain \\(\\int h(x) = 1\\) and symmetry, reducing local optima risks. The linear Diophantine method (\\(0, 1, 2\\) states) provides low-complexity gradient-free optimization. Dual annealing handles high-dimensional optimization by simulating physical cooling.  \n   **Bottlenecks addressed**: Existing algorithms focus on local optima or fixed resolutions. We solve this by global optimization and resolution-dependent search, but face trade-offs in higher dimensions.  \n\n2. **Mathematical specification**  \n   - **Variables**: \\(g \\in \\mathbb{R}^{212}\\), defining an anti-palindromic function \\(\\boxed{h}_{425}(x) = [\\texttt{antipal}_h(g)]\\) for \\(5 \\leq n \\text{ odd}\\).  \n   - **Objectives**: Minimize \\(\\boxed{\\text{score}} = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) \\, dx\\) approximated by \\(\\boxed{\\text{corr}_{\\max}} \\cdot (2/n)\\) via `np.correlate`.  \n   - **Constraints**: \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\), enforced by projection (Section 4). The grid length \\(n\\) must be odd (\\(\\geq 5\\)), derived from \\(m\\) via \\(n = 2m + 1\\).  \n\n3. **Implementation procedure**  \n   - **Phase 1**: Extract \\(m\\) from input resolution, define \\(\\boxed{\\text{objective}}(g) = \\max(\\texttt{np.correlate}(\\text{antipal}}_h(g), 1 - \\text{antipal}}_h(g))) \\cdot (2/n)\\).  \n   - **Phase 2**: Use `dual_annealing` with the objective, bounds \\([0,1]^{212}\\), and initial point from `initial_h_values[:212]\\).  \n   - **Phase 3**: Project accepted \\(g\\) to \\([0,1]\\), build \\(h\\), and validate.  \n   - **Gradients**: Not used due to non-smoothness; handle optimization via Diophantine state predictions.  \n\n4. **Search choices**  \n   - **Dual Annealing**: Temperature schedule \\(T_{\\text{init}} = 100\\), \\(T_{\\text{final}} = 0.1\\), maximum function evaluations \\(N_{\\text{evals}} = \\min(3000, \\text{budget}) / \\text{rand}\\).  \n   - **Resolution**: \\(n = 5\\) to \\(625\\), scaled for gradient-free search.  \n   - **Restart**: Scale \\(x_0\\) randomly for \\(R = 3\\) iterations if no improvement \\(\\delta t\\).  \n\n5. **Budget and robustness**  \n   Time dominated by dual annealing evaluations (~\\(100\\) evaluations per \\(n\\)). Monitor total operations (`n\\) points \\(\\times\\) \\(`2\\) states\\(), for \\(N_{\\text{evals}}}^{approx}\\). For \\(n=425\\), budget \\(1300\\) seconds = ~\\(130\\) evaluations.  \n\n6. **Validation**  \n   - **Feasibility**: Check \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) via `project_feasible` (clipping + L2 projection).  \n   - **Fallback**: If \\(\\text{score} \\geq 0.3809\\), return parent solution interpolated at \\(n\\) points.  \n   - **Output**: \\(\\boxed{(h, \\text{score}, n)}\\), where `h` is anti-palindromic.",
